// C1.7.4b / ADR 0016 D6b over ADR 0015 D11, register D42: WIDENING a generic-struct
// instance into a nullable — `let some: ?Bx<i64> = b;`. ADR 0015 D11 heap-boxes a
// `?Struct` payload (alloc + store + pointer payload) and ADR 0016 D6b extended
// `NullableInner` to instances, so `?Bx<i64>` takes the same box.
//
// inkwell implemented the box for `Struct` and let `GenericInstance` fall to the inline
// branch, which passed a `%Bx_i64` VALUE into a slot `llvm_basic_type` types as `ptr`.
// This program therefore did not COMPILE: it type-checked and then died inside
// `sentinel-codegen`'s own `verify()` with "Invalid InsertValueInst operands!
// %widen_payload = insertvalue { i1, ptr } { i1 true, ptr undef }, %Bx_i64 %b1, 1".
// The polarity control is what shows it was a missing arm and not a deferral: the
// identical `?Struct` widen (`let op: ?P = p;`) built and ran throughout.
//
// ⚠ WHY THIS FIXTURE ASSERTS ONLY `is_some`, AND NOT THE PAYLOAD. You cannot read a
// heap-indirect nullable payload back in ANY of the three emitters: `unwrap_or` on a
// `?Struct` or `?GI` feeds the raw `ptr` into a `select` typed at the payload struct
// with no load through the box. That is register D47, and it is OLDER and WIDER than
// D42 — it applies to `?Struct`, nominally supported since C1.6. So D42 completes the
// CONSTRUCTION half only, which is exactly what this file pins.
//
// ⚠ D47 OPTION A HAS SINCE LANDED, so the paragraph above describes what USED to happen
// rather than what a reader will see: `unwrap_or` on a heap-indirect payload is now
// REFUSED at the type layer, and the inkwell PANIC (`enums.rs:333`, on the unbound form
// `unwrap_or(o, d).v`) is gone. The refusal is not the feature — reading the payload
// back is still missing, and options B/C are live in `docs/open-decisions.md`. One route
// is still un-refused: a generic body sees an abstract `?T` (register D53).
//
// ⚠ THE CODEGEN DIFFERENTIAL CANNOT SEE THIS PROGRAM. The text oracle refuses the widen
// ("widen-to-nullable of a struct/generic payload (heap-box) deferred"), so it is
// skipped before `scg` runs; `scg`'s own `cg_widen` is unmirrored for the same reason.
// The pin here is `snc build` + run, through inkwell. The front-end differentials
// (lex/ast/resolve/types/borrow/effects/mir) DO compare it.
//
// The exit code carries one more assertion than it looks: the box is heap-allocated and
// scope-exit dropped (`needs_drop` is true for a `?GI`, and the drop emitter frees the
// payload when valid), so a double free or a free of a non-pointer would crash rather
// than return 42. Verified that a double free really is observable here: a hand-built
// two-`sentinel_free` program exits 0xC0000374 STATUS_HEAP_CORRUPTION.
//
// ⚠ WHAT THE EXIT CODE DOES *NOT* WITNESS: the box drop is SHALLOW. It frees the box and
// does not recurse into the payload's own heap, so a payload that owns anything leaks
// it — `struct Bx<T> { xs: [T] }` at `[i64]` measures 2 `sentinel_alloc` against 1
// `sentinel_free`, while the same struct bound WITHOUT the box measures 1 and 1. The
// `?Struct` control is byte-identical, so this is pre-existing ADR 0015 D11 behaviour
// that D42 newly lets a `?GI` reach, not something D42 introduces. Filed separately.
// This fixture's own payload owns no heap, so it is unaffected either way.
//
// 40 + 2 = 42.
struct Bx<T> { v: T }

fn main() -> i64 {
    let b: Bx<i64> = Bx { v: 40 };
    let some: ?Bx<i64> = b;
    let none: ?Bx<i64> = null;
    let a: i64 = if is_some(some) { 40 } else { 0 };
    let c: i64 = if is_some(none) { 0 } else { 2 };
    a + c
}
